graduated in wavelengths for this reason.

210
00:16:10.234 --> 00:16:13.086
Where that circle crosses
the right half of the

211
00:16:13.126 --> 00:16:16.552
real axis, the value of
r read there is the SWR.

212
00:16:16.602 --> 00:16:19.057
And because a quarter
wavelength inverts the

213
00:16:19.097 --> 00:16:21.835
impedance, the normalised
admittance is the point

214
00:16:21.875 --> 00:16:26.704
diametrically opposite the
impedance, a 180 degree rotation.

215
00:16:28.354 --> 00:16:32.071
Now the example on the slides,
step by step on the chart.

216
00:16:32.121 --> 00:16:37.615
Normalise first: 100 plus j 50 over
50 is 2 plus j 1, which we place

217
00:16:37.655 --> 00:16:42.249
at the intersection of the r equals
2 circle and the x equals 1 arc;

218
00:16:42.289 --> 00:16:49.782
analytically gamma is one plus j over
three plus j, 0.447 at 26.6 degrees.

219
00:16:49.832 --> 00:16:54.425
Draw the circle of constant gamma through
the load: it cuts the real axis on

220
00:16:54.465 --> 00:17:02.070
the right at r equal to 2.62, so the SWR
is 2.62, read as about 2.6 on the chart.

221
00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:05.104
To find the input impedance,
read the load on the

222
00:17:05.144 --> 00:17:09.732
wavelengths-towards-generator
scale, 0.213 lambda, which

223
00:17:09.772 --> 00:17:14.977
the slide rounds to 0.214,
add the line length, 0.2, and

224
00:17:15.017 --> 00:17:21.333
stop at 0.413 lambda; the point
reads 0.5 minus j 0.5, so Z

225
00:17:21.373 --> 00:17:29.684
in is about 25 minus j 25 ohms, against
24.8 minus j 24.6 from the formula.

226
00:17:29.734 --> 00:17:34.180
Finally the admittance is the
point opposite the load, 0.4

227
00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:38.666
minus j 0.2, which de-normalised,
divided by 50 ohms, is

228
00:17:38.706 --> 00:17:46.106
0.008 minus j 0.004 siemens, that
is 8 minus j 4 millisiemens.

229
00:17:47.756 --> 00:17:52.401
This is the classic problem from Pozar's
book that the slides leave for you.

230
00:17:52.451 --> 00:17:57.578
The normalised load is 0.4 plus
j 0.7, and gamma at the load

231
00:17:57.618 --> 00:18:05.990
is minus 60 plus j 70 over 140
plus j 70, 0.589 at 104 degrees.

232
00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:12.506
The SWR is 1.589 over 0.411, 3.87, and the

233
00:18:12.546 --> 00:18:18.500
return loss is minus 20
log of 0.589, 4.6 dB.

234
00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:23.774
Moving 0.3 wavelengths towards the
generator rotates gamma clockwise by twice

235
00:18:23.814 --> 00:18:34.283
360 times 0.3, 216 degrees, so gamma at
the input is 0.589 at minus 112 degrees.

236
00:18:34.333 --> 00:18:39.868
Converting back, Z in is 36.5
minus j 61.1 ohms; on the

237
00:18:39.908 --> 00:18:46.281
chart you read about 0.365
minus j 0.611, Pozar's answer.

238
00:18:47.931 --> 00:18:51.787
At high frequency impedance and
admittance matrices are difficult to

239
00:18:51.827 --> 00:18:55.625
measure directly, because they need
ideal open or short circuits at

240
00:18:55.665 --> 00:18:59.406
the ports, which are hard to realise,
so we describe networks with

241
00:18:59.446 --> 00:19:03.978
scattering parameters, which a vector
network analyser measures directly.

242
00:19:04.028 --> 00:19:08.098
At each port we define a normalised
incident wave a and a reflected

243
00:19:08.138 --> 00:19:12.024
wave b, the voltage waves divided
by the square root of the port

244
00:19:12.064 --> 00:19:16.708
impedance, and the scattering matrix
relates them: b equals S times a.

245
00:19:16.758 --> 00:19:20.887
Each parameter S i j is b i over
a j, measured with all the other

246
00:19:20.927 --> 00:19:25.896
ports terminated in matched loads, so
that nothing comes back into them.

247
00:19:25.946 --> 00:19:29.353
For a two-port, S one one and
S two two are the reflection

248
00:19:29.393 --> 00:19:33.275
coefficients at the input and the
output, S two one is the forward

249
00:19:33.315 --> 00:19:38.359
transmission, the gain of an amplifier,
and S one two the reverse transmission.

250
00:19:38.409 --> 00:19:42.636
That is why last year's exam gave
the amplifier gains as S two one

251
00:19:42.676 --> 00:19:48.198
equal to 10 dB, and why minus 20 log
of S one one is the return loss.

252
00:19:49.848 --> 00:19:54.285
Matching networks transform the
load so that the line sees Z zero.

253
00:19:54.335 --> 00:19:58.514
That delivers maximum power to the
load, improves the signal-to-noise

254
00:19:58.554 --> 00:20:02.795
ratio of a receiver when the antenna
is matched to the LNA, and in a

255
00:20:02.835 --> 00:20:08.199
transmitter minimises wasted power, saving
battery and reducing radiation hazards.

256
00:20:08.249 --> 00:20:11.766
The simplest network is the
L-section, two reactive elements,

257
00:20:11.806 --> 00:20:16.122
which is practical with lumped
components up to about 1 GHz.

258
00:20:16.172 --> 00:20:19.460
If the normalised load lies
inside the r equals one

259
00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:22.657
circle, that is if R L is
larger than Z zero, the

260
00:20:22.697 --> 00:20:27.354
element next to the load is in
shunt and the second one in series.

261
00:20:27.404 --> 00:20:31.031
If it lies outside, R L smaller
than Z zero, the element

262
00:20:31.071 --> 00:20:35.219
next to the load is in series
and the second one in shunt.

263
00:20:35.269 --> 00:20:38.711
Each case has two solutions;
de-normalise the reactance

264
00:20:38.751 --> 00:20:42.255
by multiplying by Z zero and
the susceptance by dividing

265
00:20:42.295 --> 00:20:45.293
by Z zero, and turn a
positive reactance into an

266
00:20:45.333 --> 00:20:48.648
inductor and a negative one
into a capacitor, while a

267
00:20:48.688 --> 00:20:53.278
positive susceptance is a capacitor
and a negative one an inductor.

268
00:20:53.328 --> 00:20:55.726
For real loads there is
also the quarter-wave

269
00:20:55.766 --> 00:20:59.367
transformer, which we will see
after the L-section examples.

270
00:21:01.017 --> 00:21:05.640
Now the L-section example from the
slides, which comes from Pozar.

271
00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:09.675
The normalised load is 2 minus
j, inside the r equals one

272
00:21:09.715 --> 00:21:13.291
circle, so the first element,
next to the load, is in

273
00:21:13.331 --> 00:21:19.275
shunt, and we work with
admittances: y L is 0.4 plus j 0.2.

274
00:21:19.325 --> 00:21:23.832
The susceptance comes from Pozar's
formula, X L plus or minus root R L

275
00:21:23.872 --> 00:21:28.509
over Z zero times root of R L squared
plus X L squared minus Z zero R L,

276
00:21:28.549 --> 00:21:33.576
all over R L squared plus X L squared,
which here is minus 100 plus or

277
00:21:33.616 --> 00:21:42.736
minus 244.9 over 50 000, so b is plus
0.29 or minus 0.69; on the chart,

278
00:21:42.776 --> 00:21:50.272
adding plus j 0.29 moves the point to
0.4 plus j 0.49, on the circle that

279
00:21:50.312 --> 00:21:55.533
maps to r equals one, z equal to
one minus j 1.22, and the reactance

280
00:21:55.573 --> 00:22:01.250
follows from X equals one over B plus X L
Z zero over R L minus Z zero over B R L.

281
00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:05.607
Then a series reactance of plus
j 1.22 cancels the imaginary

282
00:22:05.647 --> 00:22:09.299
part and lands at the centre;
de-normalised, the shunt element

283
00:22:09.339 --> 00:22:13.705
is a capacitor of 0.92 picofarads
and the series element an

284
00:22:13.745 --> 00:22:20.168
inductor of 38.8 nanohenries, or
39.0 if you keep more decimals.

285
00:22:20.218 --> 00:22:24.750
The second solution uses b equal
to minus 0.69 and x equal to

286
00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:30.103
minus 1.22: a shunt inductor of
46.1 nanohenries and a series

287
00:22:30.143 --> 00:22:35.847
capacitor of 2.61 picofarads, which is
the answer printed on the next slide.

288
00:22:37.497 --> 00:22:39.927
The second matching problem on the slides

289
00:22:39.967 --> 00:22:42.967
has no printed solution,
so let us solve it.

290
00:22:43.017 --> 00:22:48.563
The normalised load is 0.5 plus
j 0.3, outside the r equals one

291
00:22:48.603 --> 00:22:52.181
circle, because R L is smaller
than Z zero, so the series

292
00:22:52.221 --> 00:22:56.951
element goes next to the load and
the shunt element on the line side.

293
00:22:57.001 --> 00:23:01.590
Pozar's formulas for this case give
X equal to plus or minus the square

294
00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:06.807
root of R L times Z zero minus R L,
minus X L, that is plus or minus 25

295
00:23:06.847 --> 00:23:11.763
minus 15, and B equal to plus or minus
one over Z zero times the square

296
00:23:11.803 --> 00:23:17.761
root of Z zero minus R L over R L,
which is plus or minus 0.02 siemens.

297
00:23:17.811 --> 00:23:23.675
Solution one: X is plus 10 ohms, an
inductor of 0.80 nanohenries at 2

298
00:23:23.715 --> 00:23:30.700
GHz, and B is plus 0.02 siemens, a
shunt capacitor of 1.59 picofarads.

299
00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:35.776
Solution two: X is minus 40
ohms, a series capacitor of 1.99

300
00:23:35.816 --> 00:23:40.648
picofarads, and B is minus 0.02
siemens, a shunt inductor of

301
00:23:40.688 --> 00:23:46.494
3.98 nanohenries; substituting
back, both give exactly 50 ohms.

302
00:23:48.144 --> 00:23:51.695
For a real load, a quarter-wavelength
section of line does

303
00:23:51.735 --> 00:23:55.285
the matching on its own, because
it inverts the impedance:

304
00:23:55.325 --> 00:23:59.062
the input impedance is Z one
squared over Z L, and setting it

305
00:23:59.102 --> 00:24:03.599
equal to Z zero gives Z one equal
to the square root of Z zero Z L.

306
00:24:03.649 --> 00:24:10.547
For a 10 ohm load on a 50 ohm line, Z one
is the square root of 500, 22.36 ohms.

307
00:24:10.597 --> 00:24:14.765
Its length is a quarter of the guided
wavelength, 3 times ten to the 8

308
00:24:14.805 --> 00:24:21.466
over 4 times 3 times ten to the 9
times the square root of 4.6, 1.17 cm.

309
00:24:21.516 --> 00:24:24.681
The match is perfect only
at f zero; with a limit

310
00:24:24.721 --> 00:24:30.109
of an SWR of 1.5, the maximum
tolerable gamma is 0.2.

311
00:24:30.159 --> 00:24:35.290
Plugging it into the bandwidth formula,
the arc cosine of 0.2 over the root of

312
00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:42.769
0.96, times twice the root of 500 over
40, gives a fractional bandwidth of 0.29.

313
00:24:42.819 --> 00:24:48.954
That is 29 %, from 2.56 to 3.44
GHz, which the slides read

314
00:24:48.994 --> 00:24:53.838
from their plot as 2.55 to
3.42, as in the plot of gamma

315
00:24:53.878 --> 00:24:57.821
against frequency, where the curve
stays under the dashed limit.

316
00:24:59.471 --> 00:25:03.524
The problem that follows on the
slides uses the same three steps.

317
00:25:03.574 --> 00:25:10.391
Z one is the square root of
100 times 350, 187.1 ohms.

318
00:25:10.441 --> 00:25:18.697
At 4 GHz with an effective permittivity of
4.6, the quarter wavelength is 0.874 cm.

319
00:25:18.747 --> 00:25:22.395
An SWR of 2 allows a gamma of one third.

320
00:25:22.445 --> 00:25:26.213
The bandwidth formula then
gives 0.71, a fractional

321
00:25:26.253 --> 00:25:30.135
bandwidth of 71 %: much wider
than before, because the

322
00:25:30.175 --> 00:25:34.219
load is closer to the line impedance
and the tolerance is looser.

323
00:25:35.869 --> 00:25:38.692
Before the summary, the
traps in this unit.

324
00:25:38.742 --> 00:25:41.509
Beta l uses the guided wavelength, not the

325
00:25:41.549 --> 00:25:45.977
free-space one, and everything
repeats every half wavelength.

326
00:25:46.027 --> 00:25:50.314
Gamma rotates by twice beta
l, that is 720 degrees per

327
00:25:50.354 --> 00:25:54.051
wavelength, clockwise when you
move towards the generator.

328
00:25:54.101 --> 00:25:59.908
From the SWR, gamma is SWR minus one
over SWR plus one; the reflected power

329
00:25:59.948 --> 00:26:06.723
fraction is gamma squared, and the return
loss is minus 20 log of gamma, not 10 log.

330
00:26:06.773 --> 00:26:11.264
On the Smith chart, normalise before
you start and de-normalise at the

331
00:26:11.304 --> 00:26:16.120
end, multiplying impedances by Z
zero and dividing admittances by it.

332
00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:20.508
For an L-section, check where the
load is: inside the r equals one

333
00:26:20.548 --> 00:26:26.006
circle, the shunt element goes at the
load; outside, the series element does.

334
00:26:26.056 --> 00:26:29.906
And for a coaxial cable from a
datasheet, Z zero equals one over

335
00:26:29.946 --> 00:26:34.126
the phase velocity times the
capacitance is the quickest route.

336
00:26:35.776 --> 00:26:39.300
To summarise part three: the
reflection coefficient at the

337
00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:43.499
load and the input impedance
formula solve most line problems.

338
00:26:43.549 --> 00:26:48.344
The SWR, the return loss and the
insertion loss describe how good a

339
00:26:48.384 --> 00:26:55.392
match is, and a reflected power of 10
% corresponds to an SWR of about 1.9.

340
00:26:55.442 --> 00:26:58.845
A coaxial cable's impedance
follows from its diameters

341
00:26:58.885 --> 00:27:03.060
and dielectric, or from its
velocity factor and capacitance.

342
00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:06.877
On the Smith chart, move clockwise
towards the generator, half a

343
00:27:06.917 --> 00:27:11.193
wavelength per turn, and find the
admittance on the opposite side.

344
00:27:11.243 --> 00:27:14.912
To match a load, use an L-section
of two reactances, or a

345
00:27:14.952 --> 00:27:20.184
quarter-wave transformer with Z one
equal to the square root of Z zero Z L.

346
00:27:20.234 --> 00:27:23.889
To prepare, redo last year's
ten problems against the clock,

347
00:27:23.929 --> 00:27:27.338
about six minutes each, then
the problems on the slides,

348
00:27:27.378 --> 00:27:32.101
checking every number against these
three videos; good luck on Tuesday.
